# Students bucket

Per-student exercise sheets only. Shared lessons live on the learning path (`hub/path.mjs`): link to them, do not copy them here.

## Layout (fixed)

```text
students/<student>/README.md                          the student's room: list of subjects
  <subject>/README.md                                 the subject: list of folders
    exercises/README.md                               table of exercises
      exercise-<NN>-<slug>/README.md                  story, every new idea explained, tasks table with status, words to know
        task-<NN>-<k>-<slug>/README.md                the task from the sheet, simply said, test examples, helpers, done when
          my-solution/                                the student's desk: README only (which file to create, how to run it). No code
          solution/                                   README (walkthrough, real test output, common mistakes) + sample solution
```

- Task ids are written `04_1`, folders `task-04-1-<slug>`. Status lives in the exercise README table: `todo` → `in progress` → `done`.
- Create exercises and tasks only with `/new-exercise` or `make new-exercise` (`students/scripts/new-exercise.mjs`) so every index page and the landing page stay in sync.
- Templates: `students/_template/`. Change the template, not every task, when the format changes.
- `my-solution/` holds no code at all: the student starts alone, from an empty file. Only its `README.md` (file and class name to create, run commands, a place for written answers). `solution/` is the only place for code.
- Every folder has a `README.md`.

## Writing style

- English only: text, folder names, code identifiers, comments and program output.
- Explain like for a 6-year-old: short sentences, one idea per paragraph, real-life picture first, code second.
- A sheet in another language is translated into English faithfully: same meaning, every number and value unchanged. "Words to know" explains the English terms; list a term from the paper sheet next to its English meaning only when it really helps the student match the paper (prefer English only).
- Solutions use only what the exercise teaches. No clever tricks a beginner has not seen.

## Helping a student

Students use `/next` → `/stuck` → `/check` → `/done`. How to find their task, what you may read and the "never give the solution" rules are in [`tutor.mdc`](./tutor.mdc).
